From 3d043a9abb5ae86014a407937338150a78d4ee7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 14:20:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(kanban): durable supervisor requests for worker graph
 edits [daveey]

A dispatcher worker may only unlink its own card's edges, and assignment
routing only hands work to another task-scoped worker (and returns
no_capable_supervisor when creator == assignee profile), so a worker that
created a graph of cards had no supported way to re-order it.

kanban_supervisor_request files a durable, deduplicated request: unlink ops
between the requester's card and cards its own task created (creator_task_id
scope, re-checked at execution), from a live dispatcher worker run only. The
dispatcher tick (a non-worker process) or `hermes kanban supervisor-requests
--drain` accepts it under a lease (busy holders keep it queued; a dead holder
or expired lease redelivers; capped at 5 deliveries), applies the ops through
the ordinary kanban_unlink handler, and records correlated
supervisor_request_{queued,accepted,done,rejected,failed} events plus a comment
on the requester, waking it when parked (also when it parks after the result).
A result never completes, unblocks or releases the requester.

Worker sibling unlink stays refused (tool and, newly, the CLI from a worker
shell) and now names the route. Link/unlink handlers move to
tools/kanban_tools_graph.py so the facade does not grow.
